{"parentUuid":"db6c9407-6969-4b6e-a91c-c41ed789036c","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app/workspace/data","sessionId":"38c7d341-6530-47d7-8864-d8ab21466574","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a2ead08","slug":"adaptive-exploring-penguin","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_0122cLWoqZtik6VX14cYjoq2","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Task Event\" \"Job Event\" Google cluster schema columns \"event type\" \"priority\" \"cpu request\"\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"cluster-data/ClusterData2011_2.md at master · google/cluster-data\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/cluster-data/blob/master/ClusterData2011_2.md\"},{\"title\":\"First Steps Exploring the Google Cluster dataset with iPython | Mark Stillwell\",\"url\":\"https://stillwell.me/~mark/post/first-steps-exploring-the-google-cluster-dataset-with-ipython/\"},{\"title\":\"Analysis and Clustering of Workload in Google\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1501.01426\"},{\"title\":\"Event types | Cloud Monitoring | Google Cloud\",\"url\":\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/dashboards/event-types\"},{\"title\":\"Google Cluster Trace Dataset Overview\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/google-cluster-trace-dataset\"},{\"title\":\"Heterogeneity and Dynamicity of Clouds at Scale: Google Trace Analysis\",\"url\":\"https://amplab.cs.berkeley.edu/wp-content/uploads/2012/11/socc89-screen.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Towards understanding heterogeneous clouds at scale: Google trace analysis\",\"url\":\"https://www.pdl.cmu.edu/PDL-FTP/CloudComputing/ISTC-CC-TR-12-101.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - google/cluster-scheduler-simulator: Automatically exported from code.google.com/p/cluster-scheduler-simulator\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/cluster-scheduler-simulator\"},{\"title\":\"View cluster autoscaler events | Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) | Google Cloud\",\"url\":\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/cluster-autoscaler-visibility\"},{\"title\":\"Priority-based scheduling in gke | Google Cloud Blog\",\"url\":\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/priority-based-scheduling-in-gke/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found detailed information about the Google cluster schema columns for Task and Job events:\n\n## Task Events Schema\n\nThe task events CSV includes columns such as 'time', 'missing', 'job_id', 'task_idx', 'machine_id', 'event_type', 'user', 'sched_cls', 'priority', 'cpu_requested', 'mem_requested', 'disk', 'restriction'.\n\n## Job Events and Task Characteristics\n\nThe dataset includes six principal CSV tables: machine events, machine attributes, job events, task events, task constraints, and task (instance) resource usage.\n\nEach task is specified with various parameters, including priority, resource request (estimated maximum RAM and CPU needed), and, sometimes, constraints.\n\n## Priority System\n\nPriorities in the trace range from 0 to 11 inclusive; bigger numbers mean \"more important\". 0 and 1 are \"free\" priorities; 9, 10, and 11 are \"production\" priorities; and 12 is a \"monitoring\" priority.\n\n## Data Collection\n\nSampling frequencies are event-driven for lifecycle transitions (state changes per machine, job, or task) and periodic for resource usage metrics (fixed intervals, typically 5 minutes).\n\nThe search results provide comprehensive information about the Google cluster schema structure, including the specific columns you asked about: event_type, priority, and cpu_requested are all standard fields in the task events table.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"0037c703-8e85-42a5-851d-df76bb3200e3","timestamp":"2026-01-29T06:37:45.282Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"db6c9407-6969-4b6e-a91c-c41ed789036c"}
